VI  - 2015
DP  - 2015
TI  - Successful Treatment of Bulla with Endobronchial Valves.
PG  - 947403
LID - 10.1155/2015/947403 [doi]
LID - 947403
AB  - Emphysematous bullae are a complication of end-stage COPD. Patients with large 
      bullae and poor respiratory function have limited treatment options. Surgical 
      resection is a recognized treatment, but functional improvement after bullectomy 
      is not satisfactory in patients with forced expiratory volume in 1 s (FEV1) < 35% 
      predicted. When this 59-year-old male end-stage COPD patient was assessed, he was 
      cachectic and lung function tests showed a FEV1 of 0.56 L (19% predicted) and a 
      RV of 7 L (314% predicted), while 6MWT was 315 m and MRC dyspnea score was 4. 
      Chest X-ray revealed a massive bulla of 10 cm in diameter in the right middle 
      lobe. A fibrobronchoscopy was performed under local anesthesia and 2 Zephyr 4.0 
      valves were placed in the right middle lobe. Chest X-ray and CT scan performed 36 
      days later showed the complete resolution of the bulla. Seven months later, the 
      patient demonstrated an improvement in FEV1 (+30%) and a decrease in RV from 314 
      to 262% predicted. This case report shows that the Zephyr valves may be 
      successfully used to treat a large bulla in the right middle lobe in a patient 
      with diffuse emphysema and severely impaired lung function.
