DP  - 2026 Aug
TI  - Lung Cancer in the Collapsed Lobe Following Successful Bronchoscopic Lung Volume 
      Reduction.
PG  - e250601
LID - 10.1148/ryct.250601 [doi]
AB  - In bronchoscopic lung volume reduction (BLVR), one-way endobronchial valves 
      induce atelectasis of a target lobe, resulting in lung volume reduction in 
      patients with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Despite improvement 
      in overall quality of life, these patients remain at high risk for lung cancer, 
      including within the collapsed lobe, where nodule detection is particularly 
      challenging. This article presents a case of lung cancer arising in a collapsed 
      lobe after BLVR and highlights imaging features suggestive of a growing 
      malignancy. Although a nodule may be visible at noncontrast CT, contrast-enhanced 
      chest CT can improve nodule conspicuity in this setting. Keywords: Pulmonary, CT, 
      Lung, Tracheobronchial Tree, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, 
      Neoplasms-Primary Supplemental material is available for this article. © RSNA, 
      2026.
